<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<worksheet margin="0.5in" bottom="0.75in" xml:id="progress_check_1" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>Progress Check 1</title>
  <page>
          <exercise workspace="1.5in" xml:id="pc1_london_eye">
                  <introduction>
                          <p>
                                  The London Eye is a <q>cantilevered observation wheel</q> (not quite the same as a Ferris wheel, but close) that stands on the South Bank of the Thames in London, England. Here are some facts about the London Eye, and some questions for you to answer.
                                  </p>
                                  <figure xml:id='pc1_fig_london_eye'>
                                          <caption>A Clipart image of the London Eye</caption>
                                  <image width="45%" source="london-eye_23-2147514691.jpg" >
                                  <description>
                                          <p>
                                                  A Clipart image of the London Eye
                                          </p>
                                  </description>
                          </image>
                                  </figure>
                                  <p>
                                          <ul>
                                                  <li>
                                   The London Eye is designed so that it turns slowly enough that riders can get on and off without the wheel ever having to stop.
                                                  </li>
                                                  <li>
                                                          The London Eye has 32 passenger <q>pods</q>, numbered 1<ndash/>12, 14<ndash/>33, skipping <q>unlucky</q> 13.
                                                  </li>
                                                  <li>
                                                          The London Eye has a diameter of <m>135</m><nbsp/>meters.
                                                  </li>
                                                  <li>
                                                                  The London Eye rotates so that the speed with which the pods move around the Eye is <m>26\text{cm}/\text{s}</m>.
                                                  </li>
                                          </ul>
                          </p>
                  </introduction>
                  <task>
                          <statement>
                                  <p>
                                          How long does it take the wheel to make one complete revolution? Give your answer in seconds and in terms that are meaningful for the average person (for example, the average person would not say that it takes them 3600 seconds to drive to Kalamazoo).
                                  </p>
                          </statement>
                          <solution>
                                  <p>
                                          The circumference is <m>135\pi</m><nbsp/>meters, and the speed the wheel travels is <m>26\text{cm}/s=0.26\text{m}/\text{s}</m>, so the time it takes for one revolution is
                                                  <md>
                                                          <mrow>\frac{135\pi\text{m}}{0.26\text{m}/\text{s}}&amp;\approx 1631.21157\text{s}</mrow>
                                                          <mrow>&amp;\approx 27.187\text{min}</mrow>
                                                  </md>
                                          </p>
                          </solution>
                  </task>
                  <task>
                          <statement>
                                  <p>
                                          If you miss one pod, how long do you have to wait for the next one?
                                  </p>
                          </statement>
                          <solution>
                                  <p>
                                                  <md>
                                                          <mrow>
                                                          \frac{1631.21157\text{s}}{32}&amp;\approx 50.98\text{s}
                                                          </mrow>
                                                  </md>
                                  </p>
                          </solution>
                  </task>
                  <task>
                          <introduction>
                                  <p>
                                          Assume that you get on the Eye at time <m>t=0\text{s}</m>. 
                                  </p>
                          </introduction>
                          <task>
                          <statement>
                                  <p>
                                          Find a sinusoidal model of the form <m>h(t)=a\sin\left(\omega(t-\phi)\right)+d</m>, that gives your height, in meters, as a function of time, in seconds.
                                  </p>
                          </statement>
                          <solution>
                                  <p>
                                          We know that the London Eye is <m>135</m>m tall, and we can assume the bottom is at ground level, so <m>\text{max}=135</m> and <m>\text{min}=0</m>, which gives us 
                                          <md>
                                                  <mrow>a&amp;=\frac{\text{max}-\text{min}}{2}&amp;d&amp;=\frac{\text{max}+\text{min}}{2}</mrow>
                                                  <mrow> &amp;=\frac{135-0}{2}&amp; &amp;=\frac{135+0}{2}</mrow>
                                                  <mrow> &amp;=67.5&amp;d&amp;=67.5</mrow>
                                          </md>
                                  </p>
                                  <p>
                                                  We also know that the period is <m>P=1631.21157\text{s}</m>, so 
                                                  <md>
                                                          <mrow> \omega&amp;=\frac{2\pi}{P} </mrow>
                                                          <mrow>       &amp;=\frac{2\pi}{1631.21157} </mrow>
                                                          <mrow>       &amp;\approx 0.00385185185</mrow>
                                                  </md>
                                  </p>
                                  <p>
                                          Finally, we want the sinusoid to start at its minimum (<m>0</m>m), but sine starts at the midline (increasing). To move the minimum to <m>t=0</m>, we need to either shift left by <m>3/4</m> of the period or shift right by <m>1/4</m> of the period. Thus, we can choose either <m>\phi=P/4</m> or <m>\phi=-3P/4</m>. We will use <m>\phi=P/4\approx 407.8028925</m>. So, our sinusoid is
                                          <md>h(t)=67.5\sin\big(0.00385185185(t-407.8028925)\big)+67.5</md>
                                  </p>
                          </solution>
                          </task>
                          <task>
                                  <statement>
                                          <p>
                                                  How high will you be after 5 minutes? After 10 minutes? After 15 minutes?
                                          </p>
                                  </statement>
                                  <solution>
                                          <p>
                                                  First, we note that <m>5\text{min}=300\text{s}</m>, <m>10\text{min}=600\text{s}</m>, and <m>15\text{min}=900\text{s}</m>. So, we can evaluate our function, <m>h(t)</m> at each of these times to get the height:
                                                  <md>
                                                          <mrow>
                                                                  h(300\text{s})&amp;=40.269808\text{m}&amp;
                                                                  h(600\text{s})&amp;=113.03012\text{m}&amp;
                                                                  h(900\text{s})&amp;=131.46484\text{m}
                                                          </mrow>
                                                  </md>
                                          </p>
                                  </solution>
                          </task>
                  </task>
          </exercise>
  </page>
  <page>
          <exercise workspace="0.5in" xml:id="pc1_plane">
                  <introduction>
                          <p>
                                  You can see a plane in the air coming toward you. You know (somehow) that the plane is flying at a cruising altitude of <m>5\text{km}=5000\text{m}</m>. <xref ref="pc1_figure_plane" text="type-hybrid" /> depicts the plane at two different times along with angles of elevation for each position.
                          </p>
                          <figure xml:id="pc1_figure_plane">
                                  <caption>A Plane at Height 5km at Two Different Times</caption>
                                  <image width="50%">
                                          <description>
                                                  <p>
                                                  A diagram showing the height and angles of elevation of a plane. The height is constant 5km and the angles of elevation are theta 1 = 0.785 radians and theta 2 = 0.968 radians. 
                                                  </p>
                                          </description>
                                          <xi:include href="./plane.xml"/>
                                  </image>
                          </figure>
                  </introduction>
                  <task>
                          <statement>
                                  <p>
                                                  When you first notice the plane, it has an angle of elevation of <m>\theta_{1}=0.785</m><nbsp/>radians. How far away, horizontally, is the plane from you at that time?
                                  </p>
                          </statement>
                          <solution>
                                  <p>
                                          Let <m>x_{1}</m> be the horizontal distance for the first measurement. Then from the unit circle and similar triangles, we know that 
<md>\frac{\cos\left(\theta_{1}\right)}{x_{1}}=\frac{\sin\left(\theta_{1}\right)}{5000\text{m}}</md>
                                          so,
                                <md>
                                        <mrow>{x_{1}}&amp;=\frac{\cos\left(\theta_{1}\right)}{\sin\left(\theta_{1}\right)}{5000\text{m}}</mrow>
                                        <mrow>       &amp;=\frac{\cos\left(0.785\right)}{\sin\left(0.785\right)}{5000\text{m}}</mrow>
                                        <mrow>&amp;\approx{}5003.98\text{m}</mrow>
                                </md>
                                  </p>
                          </solution>
                  </task>
                  <task>
                          <statement>
                                  <p>
                                                  When you look at the plane five seconds later, it has an angle of elevation of <m>\theta_{2}=0.968</m><nbsp/>radians. How far away, horizontally, is the plane from you at that time?
                                  </p>
                          </statement>
                          <solution>
                                  <p>
                                          Let <m>x_{2}</m> be the horizontal distance for the second measurement. Then from the unit circle and similar triangles, we know that 
<md>\frac{\cos\left(\theta_{2}\right)}{x_{2}}=\frac{\sin\left(\theta_{2}\right)}{5000\text{m}}</md>
                                          so,
                                <md>
                                        <mrow>{x_{2}}&amp;=\frac{\cos\left(\theta_{2}\right)}{\sin\left(\theta_{2}\right)}{5000\text{m}}</mrow>
                                        <mrow>&amp;=\frac{\cos\left(0.968\right)}{\sin\left(0.968\right)}{5000\text{m}}</mrow>
                                        <mrow>&amp;\approx{}3441.25\text{m}</mrow>
                                </md>
                                  </p>
                          </solution>
                  </task>
                  <task>
                          <statement>
                                  <p>
                                          Based on your previous calculations, how fast, in meters per second, is the plane flying?
                                  </p>
                          </statement>
                          <solution>
                                  <p>
                                                  The distance travelled was <m>5003.98\text{m}-3441.25\text{m}=1562.73\text{m}</m>, and the elapsed time was <m>5\text{s}</m>, so the speed was <m>1562.73\text{m}/5\text{s}=312.547\text{m}/\text{s}</m>.
                                  </p>
                          </solution>
                  </task>
          </exercise>
  </page>
  <page>
          <exercise workspace="0.65in">
                  <introduction>
                          <p>
                                  <xref ref="pc1_fig_vectors" text="type-hybrid"/> shows a pendulum along with vectors representing forces acting on the pendulum bob. The vector <m>\vec{F}_{g}=\left\langle{}0,-32\right\rangle{}</m> represents the force due to gravity of <m>32</m><nbsp/>pounds in the downward direction, while the vectors <m>\vec{F}_{R}</m> and <m>\vec{F}_{T}</m> represent the radial and tangential components of <m>\vec{F}_{g}</m>, respectively. That is, <m>\vec{F}_{R}</m> represents the part of the force from gravity that pulls against the pendulum string (and is balanced by an equal and opposite force that the string exerts) and <m>\vec{F}_{T}</m> is the part of the force from gravity that tends to move the pendulum along its path, and <m>\vec{F}_{T}+\vec{F}_{R}=\vec{F}_{g}</m>.
                          </p>
                          <figure xml:id="pc1_fig_vectors">
                                  <caption>Force Vectors Acting on a Pendulum</caption>
                                  <image width="50%">
                                          <description>
                                                  <p>
                                                          A diagram showing a pendulum at an angle of pi / 3 left from vertical, and three force vectors drawn with their tails at the pendulum bob. They are the force vector F sub g pointing downward from the pendulum bob, the force vector F sub T perpendicular to the pendulum's string and thus pointing right and downwards and the force vector F sub R pointing in the direction opposite of the pendulum's pivot point. The vectors F sub R and F sub T are the radial and tangential components of F sub g.
                                                  </p>
                                          </description>
                                          <xi:include href="./vectors.xml"/>
                                  </image>
                          </figure>
                  </introduction>
                  <task>
                          <statement>
                                  <p>
                                                  Use geometry and the fact that <m>\vec{F}_{R}</m> and <m>\vec{F}_{T}</m> are perpendicular to find angles <m>\theta_{1}</m> and <m>\theta_{2}</m>.
                                  </p>
                          </statement>
                          <solution>
                                  <p>
                                                  Because <m>\vec{F}_{R}</m> is pointing in the same direction as the pendulum string, we know that <m>\theta_{2}=\pi/3</m>, and because <m>\theta_{1}</m> and <m>\theta_{2}</m> are perpendicular to each other, <m>\theta_{1}+\theta_{2}=\pi/2</m>, so we know that <m>\theta_{2}=\pi/6</m>.
                                  </p>
                          </solution>
                  </task>
                  <task xml:id="pc1_vectors_lengths" workspace="1in">
                          <statement>
                                  <p>
                                                  Draw a copy of <m>\vec{F}_{T}</m> with its tail at the head of <m>\vec{F}_{R}</m> to form a right triangle and use trigonometry to find the lengths of <m>\vec{F}_{T}</m> and <m>\vec{F}_{R}</m>. Call these lengths <m>\ell_{T}</m> and <m>\ell_{R}</m>.
                                  </p>
                          </statement>
                          <solution>
                                  <p>
                                                  We can see that <m>\frac{\ell_{R}}{\cos(\theta_{2})}=\frac{32}{1}</m>, so <m>\ell_{R}=32\cos\left(\pi/3\right)=32\cdot\frac{1}{2}=16</m>, and similarly, <m>\frac{\ell_{T}}{\sin(\theta_{2})}=\frac{32}{1}</m>,  so <m>\ell_{T}=32\sin\left(\pi/3\right)=32\sqrt{3}/2=16\sqrt{3}\approx 27.7128</m>.
                                  </p>
                          </solution>
                  </task>
                  <task xml:id="pc1_vectors_direction_R">
                          <statement>
                                  <p>
                                                  Use your knowledge of the unit circle to find a vector of length <m>1</m> pointing in the direction of <m>\vec{F}_{R}</m>. Call this vector <m>\vec{u}_{R}</m>.
                                  </p>
                          </statement>
                          <hint>
                                  <p>
                                                  You can draw the unit circle with its center at the pendulum bob.
                                  </p>
                          </hint>
                          <solution>
                                  <p>
                                          If we put the origin at the pendulum bob, then the angle from the positive <m>x</m>-axis to the <m>\vec{F}_{R}</m> is <m>\pi/2+\pi/3=5\pi/6</m>, but the angle is clockwise, so the point on the unit circle at that angle would be
                                          <md>
                                                  <mrow>
                                                          \vec{u}_{R}&amp;=\left[\begin{array}{r}\cos\left(\frac{-5\pi}{6}\right)\\[1.5ex]\sin\left(\frac{-5\pi}{6}\right)\end{array}\right]
                                                  </mrow>
                                                  <mrow>
                                                                  &amp;=\left[\begin{array}{r}\frac{-\sqrt{3}}{2}\\[1.5ex]\frac{-1}{2}\end{array}\right]
                                                  </mrow>
                                          </md>
                                  </p>
                          </solution>
                  </task>
                  <task xml:id="pc1_vectors_direction_T">
                          <statement>
                                  <p>
                                                  Use geometry and your knowledge of the unit circle to find a vector of length <m>1</m> pointing in the direction of <m>\vec{F}_{T}</m>. Call this vector <m>\vec{u}_{T}</m>.
                                  </p>
                          </statement>
                          <solution>
                                  <p>
                                          If we again put the origin at the pendulum bob, then the angle from the positive <m>x</m>-axis to the <m>\vec{F}_{T}</m> is <m>-\pi/2+\pi/6=-\pi/3</m>, so the point on the unit circle at that angle would be
                                          <md>
                                                  <mrow>
                                                          \vec{u}_{T}&amp;=\left[\begin{array}{r}\cos\left(\frac{-\pi}{3}\right)\\[1.5ex]\sin\left(\frac{-\pi}{3}\right)\end{array}\right]
                                                  </mrow>
                                                  <mrow>
                                                                  &amp;=\left[\begin{array}{r}\frac{1}{2}\\[1.5ex]\frac{-\sqrt{3}}{2}\end{array}\right]
                                                  </mrow>
                                          </md>
                                  </p>
                          </solution>
                  </task>
                  <task>
                          <statement>
                                  <p>
                                                  Using the lengths <m>\ell_{R}</m> and <m>\ell_{T}</m> that you found in <xref ref="pc1_vectors_lengths" text='type-local'>Part</xref> and the unit vectors <m>\vec{u}_{R}</m> and <m>\vec{u}_{T}</m> that you found in <xref ref="pc1_vectors_direction_R" text='type-local'>Part</xref> and <xref ref="pc1_vectors_direction_T" text='type-local'>Part</xref>, find <m>\vec{F}_{R}</m> and <m>\vec{F}_{T}</m>. Be sure to verify that <m>\vec{F}_{T}+\vec{F}_{R}=\vec{F}_{g}</m>!
                                  </p>
                          </statement>
                          <solution>
                                  <p>
                                          <md>
                                                  <mrow>
                                                          \vec{F}_{R}&amp;=\ell_{R}\vec{u}_{R}
                                                          &amp;
                                                          \vec{F}_{T}&amp;=\ell_{R}\vec{u}_{R}
                                                  </mrow>
                                                  <mrow>
                                                                  &amp;=16\left[\begin{array}{r} \frac{-\sqrt{3}}{2}\\ \frac{-1}{2}\end{array}\right]
                                                          &amp;
                                                                  &amp;=16\sqrt{3}\left[\begin{array}{r} \frac{1}{2}\\\frac{-\sqrt{3}}{2}\end{array}\right]
                                                  </mrow>
                                                  <mrow>
                                                                     &amp;=\left[\begin{array}{r} -8\sqrt{3}\\ -8\end{array}\right]
                                                          &amp;
                                                                     &amp;=\left[\begin{array}{r} 8\sqrt{3}\\-24\end{array}\right]
                                                  </mrow>
                                          </md>
                                                  We then verify that <m>\vec{F}_{R}+\vec{F}_{T}=\vec{F}_{g}</m>:
                                                  <md>
                                                          <mrow>
                                                                  \vec{F}_{R}+\vec{F}_{T}
                                                          &amp;=
                                                          \left[\begin{array}{r} -8\sqrt{3}\\ -8\end{array}\right]
                                                          +\left[\begin{array}{r}-8\sqrt{3}\\-24\end{array}\right]
                                                          </mrow>
                                                          <mrow>
                                                          &amp;=
                                                                  \left[\begin{array}{r}
                                                                  -8\sqrt{3}+8\sqrt{3} \\
                                                                  -8-24
                                                                  \end{array}\right]
                                                          </mrow>
                                                          <mrow>
                                                          &amp;=
                                                                  \left[\begin{array}{r}
                                                                  0 \\
                                                                  -32
                                                                  \end{array}\right]
                                                          </mrow>
                                                          <mrow>
                                                          &amp;=\vec{F}_{g}
                                                          </mrow>
                                                  </md>
                                  </p>
                          </solution>
                  </task>
          </exercise>
  </page>
</worksheet>
